.TH CHTTPCLIENT_SET_MAX_RESPONSE_BODY_SIZE 3 "2026-07-30" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpclient_set_max_response_body_size \- cap the buffered response body size
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpclient.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpclient_set_max_response_body_size(chttpcli cli,
                                                      size_t max_bytes);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Caps the buffered response body size across all three tiers:
chttpclient_do, chttpclient_do_async (and the pooled-sync wrappers
built on it, chttpclient_do_pooled), chttp_get/post/put/delete/patch,
and chttp_run_query.  Pass 0 (the default) to restore unlimited
buffering.

Has no effect on chttpclient_do_streaming, chttpclient_do_async_
streaming, or chttpclient_do_pooled_streaming: a streaming caller
already controls its own memory via chttpcli_write_fn's return
value (returning fewer bytes than len aborts the transfer), so there
is nothing for this cap to bound on that path.

Enforced two ways.  A response whose Content-Length header alone
already declares more than max_bytes is rejected immediately, before
any body byte is read off the wire.  A chunked or connection-close-
delimited body (which has no declared length to check up front) is
instead rejected reactively, the moment the cumulative body received
so far would exceed max_bytes.  Either case reports
ccol_msg_too_large from chttpclient_do / the async result's rv /
chttpclient_do_pooled, and the connection is not reused afterward
(the same as any other malformed-response failure).

Redirect hops are unaffected by this cap regardless of their own
declared or actual body size: an intermediate hop's body is always
discarded without ever being buffered, so only the final, delivered
response's body counts against max_bytes.

The up-front declared-length check only ever applies to a message
that will actually deliver the caller's body.  A 1xx informational
response's own declared Content-Length is exempt (RFC 7230 3.3.2
says a compliant server should never send one on a 1xx response, but
a misbehaving or malicious server might; such a response is always
discarded and never delivered to the caller regardless).  A HEAD
response's declared Content-Length is exempt too: it describes what
a GET would have returned (RFC 7231 4.3.2) and is never followed by
actual body bytes.

May be called before or after the first request, and takes effect
immediately for every subsequent request; an already in-flight
request is unaffected.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cli       Client handle; must not be CHTTPCLI_INVALID.
max_bytes Maximum buffered response body size in bytes; 0 = no limit.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success      Cap updated.
ccol_invalid_args cli is CHTTPCLI_INVALID, or a stale/already-
                  destroyed handle.
.fi
